Since April, people have told us their bookings land on the wrong day: a month flipped. By
4 October the desk had **412 reports**.

**Merged as one issue.** The parser reads dates month first, and our tenants write the day
first. The fix is queued.

<Callout>
Known. One issue, one fix queued.
</Callout>

## What was merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Two bugs, not one

The merge on subject counted the noise once, but the cause check (`src/lib/kb/causes.ts`)
splits these 412 reports into **two** problems with two different fixes. One ticket cannot
track two fixes, so each is filed as its own ticket:

- `parser` (**379** reports) — the shared parser reads dates month-first, flipping day-first
  (EU) tenants. [Ticket #1](https://github.com/PawPas/support-kb/issues/1); **fixed**
  (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`), closed completed.
- `hint` (**33** reports) — the `booking-v3` form hints `MM/DD/YYYY`, so day-first users type
  month-first and the form stores it wrong, before the parser runs. A different bug.
  [Ticket #2](https://github.com/PawPas/support-kb/issues/2); closed **won't fix** —
  the `booking-v3` form was retired.

The full write-up is at [#4471 · two bugs, not one](/kb/incidents/4471-causes).

## Lessons

- A big ticket is mostly one bug reported many times — but check the cause before you call it
  "known". Here it was two.
- Merge on subject to count fast; re-run the cause check before you decide the fix.
- Write the page while the queue is still in your head.

